e in the way that we talk  about it and then they pop up in Google  Translate another great example and data  said that I was impressed by how quickly  it could turn a inequality into a metric  was the ProPublica study on compass now  compass is a recidivism risk evaluation  system and what it does is it attempts  to guess how likely it is that you'll go  back to jail or prison once you've been  let out of jail or prison so it's used  in several jurisdictions within the  United States to determine along with  other metrics whether a person should be  left out on early parole and so forth  the ProPublica study said this this  machine learning algorithm this  algorithm this software is racially  biased and they showed this chart which  is a distribution chart and on the Left  there's the different scores for the  black defendants going from the left to  the right it goes to the increased risk  and from the white defendants scores  they are on the right-hand side and you  can see that there there's a majority  distribution towards the lower decile  scores  however a dong a data scientist in San  Francisco took another look at the data  and he took instead a look at the  conditional effects and here sorry the  song is a bit low is a bit small but the  probability of recidivism is on the y  axis and then the scores are along the x  axis and what we can actually see here  is the dots are fairly close together so  the algorithm or the software in and of  itself is not biased in fact we see that  some of the blue dots which represent  the white population actually have lower  probability for a higher score now what  does this tell us how can we see a  distribution like before and yet still  see that the algorithm in and of itself  is in fact skewing probability towards  in favor of the black offenders what it  tells us is maybe this metric is not so  much measuring recidivism but instead is  measuring the policing tactics of  certainly officers in the United States  and if 